The problem asks us to count the number of distinct pairs of mochi $(A_i, A_j)$ such that $A_i$ can be placed on top of $A_j$.
The condition for placing mochi $A$ (size $a$) on top of mochi $B$ (size $b$) is $a \le \frac{b}{2}$.
The mochi are given in ascending order of size: $A_1 \le A_2 \le \dots \le A_N$.
We need to find the number of pairs $(i, j)$ with $i \neq j$ such that $A_i \le \frac{A_j}{2}$.
Wait, the problem says "Two kagamimochi are distinguished if at least one of the mochi is different, even if the sizes of the mochi are the same."
This implies we are looking for the number of pairs of indices $(i, j)$ such that $A_i$ is placed on $A_j$.
However, usually "different kinds" might imply distinct pairs of sizes. Let's re-read carefully.
"Two kagamimochi are distinguished if at least one of the mochi is different, even if the sizes of the mochi are the same."
This phrasing is slightly ambiguous. Does it mean distinct pairs of indices $(i, j)$ or distinct pairs of values $(A_i, A_j)$?
